#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "297f1978-f735-52d2-810f-78acd4d35766" :question/math-academy-id "e-1488" :question/problem "A penny is projected vertically downward with an initial velocity of $300 \\, \\textrm{m/min}$ and hits the ground after $2$ seconds. Find the distance the penny covered during its fall." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n$u|= 300\\frac{m}{min}; = 300\\frac{m}{min} \\cdot \\frac{min}{60s}; = 300\\frac{m}{min} \\cdot \\frac{min}{60s}; = \\frac{300}{60} \\cdot \\frac{m}{s}; = 5m/s$\n- The penny falls for $t=2\\,\\textrm{s}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n&= (5)(2) + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 10 + 19.6 \\\\\n&= 29.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $29.6\\,\\textrm{m}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d441155e-dbcb-598d-afc7-949ee4eddbee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Time"}] :question/id #uuid "03a61226-750f-5227-9887-04b31184f5bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-1490" :question/problem "A stone is projected vertically downward from a height of $19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ with a velocity of $14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. How long will it take for the stone to hit the ground?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the stone is $u=14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$.\n- The stone covers the distance of $s=19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n19.6 &= (14.7)t + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)t^{2} \\\\\n4(4.9) &= 3(4.9)t + (4.9)t^{2} \\\\\n4(4.9) &= 3(4.9)t + (4.9)t^{2} \\\\\n4 &= 3t + t^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} + 3t - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(t + 4)(t - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -4, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the stone hits the ground after $t=1$ second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c3fb8a8-343a-5046-afce-83db17ec1039" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Initial Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "8bddae46-ab34-5d0d-b9d3-a02de1a2a72c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9738" :question/problem "A ball is projected vertically down from a building with a height of $110.4 \\, \\textrm{m}$. It takes $4\\,\\textrm{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the ball?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=4\\,\\textrm{s}$.\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=110.4\\, \\textrm{m}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n(110.4) &= u(4) + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\\n110.4 &= 4u + 78.4 \\\\\n32 &= 4u \\\\\nu &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was\n\n$$\nu= 8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892c87d5-354f-5a9b-b160-fd34961f006b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.1m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079689} {:answer/id #uuid "8b849248-9358-563f-acb7-a7eb013fef92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.4m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079690} {:answer/id #uuid "a966bd08-cf6f-5561-998b-6740f6e16629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.7m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079691} {:answer/id #uuid "f7149d03-1e4e-5c97-b17b-0d190b702855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.08m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079692} {:answer/id #uuid "4774f044-702c-5dec-8766-01eac8f9c4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88.2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892c87d5-354f-5a9b-b160-fd34961f006b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.1m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079689} :answer-field/id #uuid "222539fa-2a37-5fca-b50a-9d938dacd614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079688}] :question/id #uuid "4e3ddfe5-6a0f-5dd6-97e4-c3a65ef84d31" :question/math-academy-id "q-10586" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA ball is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $3$ seconds. Find the distance the ball covered during its fall.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8m/s^{2}$.*" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f0aabf-5329-59e7-a5e8-6db979b8699c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21da426d-ff73-55ce-bfe8-e76bbfa25fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21da426d-ff73-55ce-bfe8-e76bbfa25fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232632} :answer-field/id #uuid "547064b1-e32c-58cd-bf58-e1617027a4dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080653}] :question/id #uuid "dac6a978-302a-5e0d-9924-081014bd2660" :question/math-academy-id "q-221581" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAn object is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.9km/min$. It takes the object $4s$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8m/s^{2}$.*\n\nThe height of the building is {{field-1}} $m$.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-for-initial-velocity\"></a>" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
